---
import OffClockPage from '../../components/OffClockPage.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';
---

<OffClockPage heading="Food" description="Tanish Jain's favorite restaurants, go-to orders, and notes from a vegetarian kitchen.">
  <section class="food-section" aria-labelledby="restaurants-heading">
    <h2 id="restaurants-heading">Places I go back to</h2>
    <p class="restaurant-intro">
      These are spots I like in cities I keep coming back to. I'll save the restaurants I remember
      from other trips for <a href="/off-the-clock/travel/">Travel stories</a>.
    </p>

    <section class="entries" aria-labelledby="bay-area-heading">
      <h3 id="bay-area-heading">Bay Area</h3>
      <ul class="work">
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.hunanhouseca.com/">Hunan House</a>
          <span class="venue">Sunnyvale</span>
          <p>A friend put me onto Hunan House back when it was still quiet. These days there's often a wait, and I'd still go for the vegetable dry pot. It's properly spicy, packed with vegetables, and hearty enough to be the whole meal.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.lafiestamexicancuisine.com/menu-copy">La Fiesta</a>
          <span class="venue">Mountain View</span>
          <p>I missed San Diego's Mexican food when I moved to the Bay Area. La Fiesta is one of the places I keep going back to. I like their burritos, and if you're getting vegetarian enchiladas, try Grandma's Special sauce. It reminds me of an Indian makhani sauce.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="entries" aria-labelledby="dubai-heading">
      <h3 id="dubai-heading">Dubai</h3>
      <ul class="work">
        <li>
          <a href="https://aroosdamascus.ae/">Aroos Damascus</a>
          <p>I used to go here with my family when I lived in Dubai. It's a Dubai classic and still my go-to for Syrian food when I'm back. I remember it being pretty inexpensive back then.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.visitdubai.com/en/restaurants/mamaesh">Mama'esh</a>
          <span class="venue">Business Bay</span>
          <p>A friend who was always finding new restaurants told me about this tiny Palestinian spot in Business Bay. It was hard to find back then, and it's grown a lot since. I'd still go for the muhammara. It's the best I've had, and I still remember the smell of the bread coming out of the oven.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.zomato.com/dubai/bombay-bites-mankhool">Bombay Bites</a>
          <span class="venue">Vada pav</span>
          <p>Of all the vada pav I tried in Dubai, this came closest to what I'd get in Mumbai. I used to go to the one in the Atrium Centre on Bank Street in Mankhool.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://chatorigali.ae/our-menu/">Chatori Gali</a>
          <span class="venue">Jalebis</span>
          <p>These are the best jalebis I've had, including the ones I've had in India. They're thin, crisp, and just sweet enough.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="entries" aria-labelledby="mumbai-heading">
      <h3 id="mumbai-heading">Mumbai</h3>
      <ul class="work">
        <li>
          <a href="https://sandwizzaa.com/store-locator/">Sandwizzaa</a>
          <span class="venue">Santacruz West</span>
          <p>My go-to for a Bombay-style grilled sandwich is the original Sandwizzaa on Station Road. The menu's a lot bigger than it used to be, and I've liked most of what I've tried. I still go for the grilled sandwich. Their sandwich masala is worth picking up if you want to make one at home.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.zomato.com/mumbai/batata-vada-samrat-santacruz-west">Batata Vada Samrat</a>
          <span class="venue">Near Santacruz Station</span>
          <p>One of my favorite vada pavs in Mumbai. Get it when the vadas are coming out of the fryer. If you're between batches, wait for the next one.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Vada pav stall in Fort</strong>
          <p>My dad told me about a street vendor you could see from Flora Fountain. He made my favorite vada pav in Mumbai. I wish I remembered the name.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Frankie stall near Podar School</strong>
          <span class="venue">Santacruz</span>
          <p>I don't know the name of this stall, but the guy near Podar School makes some of the best Frankies I've had in Bombay. Get the Schezwan Frankie.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="entries" aria-labelledby="bahrain-heading">
      <h3 id="bahrain-heading">Bahrain</h3>
      <ul class="work">
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.talabat.com/bahrain/restaurant/727896/vrindavan-restaurant-manama-center?aid=1021">Vrindavan</a>
          <span class="venue">Manama</span>
          <p>This was my family's weekend spot when we lived in Bahrain. We almost always got the same thing: tandoori rotis, mushroom masala, dal tadka, and methi masala. The rotis were perfect every time, and back then the whole order came to about five dinars. We tried the chaat and pav bhaji on other visits, but this was the order we kept coming back to.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="entries" aria-labelledby="hong-kong-heading">
      <h3 id="hong-kong-heading">Hong Kong</h3>
      <ul class="work">
        <li>
          <a href="https://veggiekingdom.hk/">Veggie Kingdom</a>
          <span class="venue">Causeway Bay</span>
          <p>A friend who lives in Hong Kong put me onto this place. I usually go to the Causeway Bay one. It's my first pick for vegetarian dim sum, and I'd order pretty much anything off that part of the menu.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.discoverhongkong.com/eng/travel-guide/qts/restaurants-results/restaurants-details.id2411.%E5%8A%9F%E5%BE%B7%E6%9E%97%E4%B8%8A%E6%B5%B7%E7%B4%A0%E9%A3%9F.html">Kung Tak Lam</a>
          <span class="venue">Tsim Sha Tsui</span>
          <p>A friend's family introduced me to this one. I'd give Veggie Kingdom the edge for dim sum, but the Tsim Sha Tsui branch has a fantastic view of Victoria Harbour.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://guide.michelin.com/en/hong-kong-region/hong-kong/restaurant/lai-s-kitchen">Lai's Kitchen</a>
          <span class="venue">Wan Chai</span>
          <p>I liked everything I tried at this Cantonese spot in Wan Chai. The vegetarian mapo tofu was the standout, and it's exactly what I'd want on a cold night. The menu lists it with crispy beef, so ask the kitchen if they can make it vegetarian.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.ebeneezers.com/indian-restaurant-location/">Ebeneezer's</a>
          <span class="venue">Wan Chai</span>
          <p>I've usually ended up at this little Wan Chai spot late at night. Tandoori roti and palak paneer hit the spot.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="entries" aria-labelledby="san-diego-heading">
      <h3 id="san-diego-heading">San Diego</h3>
      <ul class="work">
        <li>
          <a href="https://vallartaexpress.com/our-menu/">Vallarta Express</a>
          <p>This was my late-night burrito spot at UC San Diego. I still make a point of going when I'm back in town. My order is the veggie burrito, enchilada style. The last part matters.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://myplumeria.com/">Plumeria Vegetarian</a>
          <p>A close friend introduced me to this vegetarian Thai spot in my last year in San Diego. I wish I'd known about it sooner. I'd go back for the noodle dishes with mock duck, especially the Pad Kee Mao.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://carolinesseasidecafe.com/">Caroline's Seaside Cafe</a>
          <span class="venue">La Jolla</span>
          <p>We used to come here for breakfast after a morning at the beach or out surfing. The food is good, and the view over the beach makes me want to linger over brunch. If you're affiliated with UC San Diego, it's worth checking whether there's a discount.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.primosmex.com/locations/la%20jolla%20cantina">Primos Mexican Food &amp; Cantina</a>
          <span class="venue">La Jolla</span>
          <p>When I was learning to surf, a friend and I would go to Primos afterward, exhausted and starving. I'd get the Monster-sized veggie burrito and go straight for the habanero salsa at the chips and salsa station. Even after surfing, I could never finish the whole burrito. Come hungry.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </section>

  <section class="food-section kitchen" aria-labelledby="kitchen-heading">
    <h2 id="kitchen-heading">From my kitchen</h2>
    <div class="bio">
      <p>Indian food is what I learned to cook first, and it's still my go-to. I got more into cooking in 2020, when I started grad school at Stanford and had a little kitchen of my own. At first I mostly made food I already knew. Over time I started making chole kulche and biryani and playing around with different kinds of chaat.</p>
      <p>I grew up in the Arabian Gulf and lived in Dubai, Bahrain, Muscat, and Doha. There was plenty of Levantine food around, and I grew up eating a lot of it. I missed it when I moved to the US, so I started trying to make some of it at home. My mom is a big reason I like doing this. She can pick up a dish she's never cooked before and get the hang of it almost immediately.</p>
      <p>Everything I cook is vegetarian. I like having a few friends over because I get to make more than one thing and hear what they'd actually eat again. They're willing guinea pigs.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Old favorites</h3>
    <dl class="recipe-notes">
      <div>
        <dt>Mexican</dt>
        <dd>I make tacos, burritos, and enchiladas pretty much the same way every time now. One thing I picked up on a trip to Mexico was tostielotes. My version is salsa verde Tostitos, corn, sour cream, and some spices. It's a quick snack, and it's still fun to make.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Lebanese</dt>
        <dd>I keep coming back to hummus, muhammara, falafel, tabbouleh, and my own version of toum. I like halloumi, and I'm still figuring out homemade labneh and what to do with it as a spread. For pita chips, I season pita with za'atar and Aleppo pepper and bake it. For dessert, I make muhallabia and Umm Ali.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <h3>What I've been trying lately</h3>
    <dl class="recipe-notes">
      <div>
        <dt>Mapo tofu</dt>
        <dd>I usually reach for Lao Gan Ma, but a friend put me onto Lee Kum Kee's Chiu Chow chili oil. The extra heat is especially good here.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Vegetable dry pot</dt>
        <dd>I started with my usual order at Hunan House. The mala sauce I brought back from China gives it some extra tingle. I always want more lotus root.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Wontons in chili oil</dt>
        <dd>I fill mine with mushrooms, carrots, cabbage, and tofu. The chili oil broth is the part I keep tinkering with.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Ramen</dt>
        <dd>If you like a creamier miso broth, try a little tahini. Peanut butter is another one I've been playing with. Blistered cherry tomatoes sautéed with garlic turned out to be a great topping.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Tteokbokki</dt>
        <dd>I like my rice cakes spicy and savory, so I go heavier on the garlic and chili and lighter on the sweetness.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Vegetarian bulgogi</dt>
        <dd>I use tofu and mushrooms in a soy, garlic, and sesame marinade. Try it in butter lettuce wraps.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Sundubu jjigae</dt>
        <dd>I keep the fermented flavor mild in my version. I portion the soft tofu gently so it holds together in the bowl.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Tangyuan</dt>
        <dd>I used black sesame and red bean paste for the fillings in these rice flour balls. A little jaggery gave the sweet syrup an earthy flavor.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Heugimja panna cotta</dt>
        <dd>I topped the black sesame panna cotta with a layer of matcha cream. I'd keep that pairing.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <footer class="kitchen-footer">
      <p>Want the full recipe? <a href={`mailto:${site.email}?subject=Recipe%20request`}>Hit me up.</a> Chai is optional. If you make the dish, I want to know what you changed.</p>
      <p>More once I remember to write down what I put in the pot.</p>
    </footer>
  </section>
</OffClockPage>
